course: world
lesson: 54
title: Sound — vibrations, loudness, pitch and echoes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every sound is made by something vibrating — moving quickly back and forth. Touch your throat while you hum and you can feel your voice box vibrating.
explain: The vibration shakes the air next to it, that air shakes the air next to it, and so the sound travels outward until it reaches our ears and makes the eardrum vibrate too.
explain: Sound needs something to travel through: air, water or solids such as wood and metal. In space there is no air, so there is no sound — astronauts talk by radio.
explain: A bigger vibration makes a louder sound; a faster vibration makes a higher sound. A short, tight guitar string vibrates fast and sounds high; a long, loose string vibrates slowly and sounds low.
explain: When sound hits a large hard surface, such as a cliff or a wall of an empty hall, it bounces back. The sound we hear coming back is called an echo.
fact: All sounds are made by vibrations.
fact: Sound travels through air, water and solids, but not through empty space.
fact: Sound travels faster through water and solids than through air.
fact: Bigger vibrations make louder sounds.
fact: Faster vibrations make higher sounds, and slower vibrations make lower sounds.
fact: Pitch means how high or low a sound is.
fact: Shorter, thinner or tighter strings make higher notes.
fact: An echo is a sound that bounces back from a hard surface.
fact: Soft materials such as curtains and carpets absorb sound and reduce echoes.
fact: Sound gets quieter the further it travels from its source.
fact: Very loud sounds can damage our hearing, so ear defenders are used near loud machines.
exercise: What makes every sound? | Something vibrating | Something getting hot | Something giving off light | Something being magnetised
exercise: What part of the ear vibrates when sound reaches it? | The eardrum | The earlobe | The ear hair | The ear wax
exercise: Why can't astronauts hear each other shout in empty space? | There is no air to carry the sound | Space is much too cold for sound to work | Their helmets are too thick to shout through | Sound travels much too fast in space to catch
exercise: What happens when you hit a drum harder? | The vibrations are bigger and the sound is louder | The sound becomes higher | The sound becomes lower | The drum stops vibrating
exercise: What does pitch mean? | How high or low a sound is | How loud or quiet a sound is | How far a sound travels | How long a sound lasts
exercise: Which guitar string gives the highest note? | A short, thin, tight string | A long, thick, loose string | A long, thin, loose string | A thick string pressed nowhere
exercise: What is an echo? | Sound bouncing back from a hard surface | A sound made by a cave itself | A sound that travels only underwater, like whale calls | A sound with no vibration
exercise: Where are you most likely to hear an echo? | In a big empty hall with hard walls | In a small bedroom full of cushions | In a soft forest of moss | Under a thick duvet
exercise: Why do music studios cover their walls with soft foam? | Soft materials absorb sound and stop echoes | Foam makes sounds higher | Foam makes the music louder | Foam keeps light out of the room
exercise: A bee buzzes with a high sound. What does that tell us about its wings? | They vibrate very fast | They vibrate very slowly | They do not vibrate at all | They vibrate only very gently
exercise: Why does a sound get quieter as you walk away from it? | The sound spreads out and weakens as it travels | The sound becomes higher | The air stops the sound completely after a short way | Your ears close a little
exercise: Through which of these does sound travel fastest? | A steel rail | The air in a room | Empty space | The air on a mountain
exercise: Why do workers with loud drills wear ear defenders? | Very loud sounds can damage hearing | Ear defenders make sounds higher | Ear defenders keep the ears warm | Drills are too quiet to hear without them
test: You press your ear to a long wooden table while a friend taps the far end. What do you notice? | You hear the tap clearly through the wood | You hear nothing, since wood blocks all sound | You hear it only after a long delay | You hear the tap as a high whistle
test: A ruler is held at the edge of a desk and twanged. The overhanging part is made shorter. What happens? | The note becomes higher | The note becomes lower | The note stays the same | The ruler makes no sound
test: Which is true about loudness? | Bigger vibrations make louder sounds | Faster vibrations make louder sounds | Loud sounds always have a high pitch | Loudness depends only on the colour of the object
test: A shout from the bottom of a canyon is heard again a moment later. Why? | The sound bounced off the rock walls | The canyon made its own sound | Another person copied the shout | Sound stops and starts again in canyons
test: Why does a gym with bare walls sound more echoey than a classroom with posters, carpets and curtains? | Soft things in the classroom absorb sound | The gym has more air in it | Posters make sounds louder | The classroom has no sound waves
test: Whales can call to each other across many kilometres of ocean. Which fact helps explain this? | Sound travels well through water | Sound cannot travel in water | Water makes sounds silent | Sound needs empty space to travel
test: A tight elastic band gives a high twang. You loosen it. What happens to the note? | It becomes lower | It becomes higher | It becomes louder only | It stays exactly the same
test: If there were no air in a room, could you hear a ringing alarm clock in it? | No, sound needs something to travel through | Yes, sound travels best in empty space | Yes, but only more quietly | Only if the clock is metal
test: why's the big drum go boom n the little one go tick | Slower vibrations of the big drum make lower sounds | The big drum vibrates faster | The small drum is louder inside | Big drums have no vibrations
test: shouted in the empty hall n heard myself back lol why | The sound bounced back from the hard walls as an echo | Someone was copying you | The sound got stuck in the air | The floor made its own sound
test: my cousin says you can hear explosions in space like in films. true? | No, sound cannot travel through empty space | Yes, sound travels best in space | Yes, but only very loud ones | No, you can only hear them if you are standing on the Moon
test: Ben reckons sound can't go through water. right? | No, sound travels through water, even faster than through air | Yes, water stops all sound | Yes, fish cannot hear anything | No, it travels only through ice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of all sound? | All sound is produced by vibrations | All sound is produced by the flow of electricity | All sound is produced by the reflection of light | All sound is produced by the motion of the wind
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the propagation of sound through a vacuum. | Sound is incapable of travelling through a vacuum | Sound travels most rapidly through a vacuum | Sound travels through a vacuum as readily as through air | Sound travels through a vacuum only at night
exam: It is given that a string is shortened and tightened. Ascertain the consequent alteration in the note produced thereby. | The pitch of the note shall be raised | The pitch of the note shall be lowered | The loudness alone shall be diminished | The note shall remain wholly unaltered
exam: Identify the principal reason for which curtains and carpets reduce echoes within a room. | Soft materials absorb sound rather than reflecting it | Soft materials reflect sound more strongly than walls | Soft materials raise the pitch of every sound | Soft materials accelerate the passage of sound
